WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
This is Goldman Sachs, where the world's

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.800
most powerful governments, corporations,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
and billionaires go to figure out what

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.800
to do with their money. Almost nobody

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.080
gets access to that advice, but I worked

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.200
there, and what I learned from the

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.200
inside changed how I build wealth

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.880
forever. These are the lessons very few

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.680
people ever get to learn.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.720
Get a minute, Donna.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.040
Number one, your first 500k is for

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.840
losing. So, I say lose somebody else's

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.760
money. Like, one of the best pieces of

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.520
advice I got at Goldman came from a

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.520
mentor I had there, and it's probably

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.080
going to piss you off cuz I kind of felt

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.760
that way at the time. He said, "Your

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.040
first half a million dollars of

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.640
investing experience is for losing."

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.610
He's like, "You will lose it. You'll buy

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.440
at the top. You'll make bets that seem

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.240
[music] obvious that are

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.160
catastrophically wrong in hindsight. So,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.160
why would you lose your own money? Why

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.160
not lose somebody else's first?" What's

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.800
cool is when you work at a big

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.640
institution, Goldman, JP Morgan,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.280
BlackRock, you make mistakes on their

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.560
dime. This is true for you at any

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.240
company when you first start out. Like,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.120
the companies don't care as much because

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.480
they budget it. It's called a risk

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.280
budget. So, they won't fire you over a

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.720
learning curve mistake. That's just the

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.080
cost of doing business at scale. But, if

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.600
you lose money, that might be your rent

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.000
or your savings, and that's when panic

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.880
decisions, they start to come and try to

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.280
claw it back, which makes it worse. The

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.280
most dangerous advice circling the

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.880
internet right now is just go be an

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.800
entrepreneur tomorrow. But,

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.760
statistically, it's actually terrible

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.480
advice for most people. 90% of startups

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.520
fail. So, if your first mistakes are

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.160
inevitable, and they are, why not do it

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.000
on somebody else's dime? Then go out on

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.840
your Goldman made me risk adverse. So,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.640
before I make any major move now, I run

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.680
it through what I call the OPX check.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.960
Three questions, you can steal them.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.840
Whose capital can I use instead of mine?

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.400
Whose resources can I use instead of

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.320
mine? Whose network can I use instead of

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.240
mine? If the answer to all three is my

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.280
own, I'm probably not thinking about it

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.480
right. So, I use this term OPX. It

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.800
stands for other people's X, where X is

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.000
whatever asset you need, money,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.680
contacts, infrastructure, credibility.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.760
Goldman never takes a position without

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.080
limiting their own exposure first. This

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.400
is how you do the same thing at any

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.400
level. That's rule number one.

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:10.640
>> [music]

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.640
>> Number two, this is a hard one. Cut your

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.959
bottom 20%. So, every year this place

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.320
fires the bottom 20% of its people. And

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:19.320
I used to think that was brutal because

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.880
it is. Until I realized every single

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.840
high performer in the world does the

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.560
exact same thing. They're just quieter

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.720
about it. Like I watched a guy, I'll

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.680
call him Jack, see himself trending

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.880
toward the bottom 20% performance at

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.120
this company. And instead of fixing his

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.080
numbers, he spent six months kind of

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.720
brilliantly engineering the appearance

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:38.160
of better numbers. Could you imagine the

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.680
amount of energy and effort spent

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.560
looking successful instead of actually

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.160
becoming it? Like it's such a waste. So,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.720
Goldman ended up firing him anyway with

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.040
a bad recommendation. And a bad

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.200
recommendation from this place is

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.800
basically like a career bomb. And you

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.959
never really walk away from it. So, like

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.880
a decade later I ran into Jack. And he

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:57.200
is, you know, not living his best life.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.440
And it wasn't actually that he lacked

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.959
talent at all. He was super smart. He

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.400
just couldn't face the decision of what

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:06.440
was happening and wasn't willing to do

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.320
the hard work instead. So, what can you

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.720
take from this? An annual audit of

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.560
everything you're carrying. Your

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.000
clients, your habits, your circle. Cut

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.880
the bottom 20% each year. Every year.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.920
So, the clients costing you energy while

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.600
you're barely moving the needle, they're

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.800
gone. The morning habits that are

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.360
deciding what your years look like, gone

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.720
if they don't help. The people in your

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.200
circle quietly capping your ceiling,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.680
well, get those out of the way. Because

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.239
every high performer has to cut to make

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.160
room to grow. Just like the flowers here

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:36.959
have to be pruned every year. The only

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.680
question is whether you're honest enough

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:41.160
to actually name what your bottom 20%

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.160
is.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.519
The third one, I guess, is be honest

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.320
even when you're about to publicly fail.

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:50.959
The most expensive mistake I watched

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.560
people make at Goldman was nothing to do

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:54.200
with a bad trade. It was about what they

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.200
chose to do the moment they realized

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.680
they were falling behind. My colleague

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.480
Jack's mistake was really not him

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.320
failing, but him lying. You know, I've

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.880
seen this pattern everywhere in life.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.400
You know, someone falls short, they know

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.040
it, and they start spinning, building

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.080
elaborate story around the gap instead

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.920
of just like, "Yeah, that is true. Now

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.480
I'm going to close the gap." So, you

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.560
know, we all manage how it looks instead

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.480
of fixing what it is. All that happens

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.519
then is you construct a house of cards

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.160
and the house falls. People are actually

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:23.280
going to have more respect for you when

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.120
you say, "I'm failing at this. Here's

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.960
why. Here's what I'm doing about it."

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.919
than they ever would have for someone

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.040
who gets caught in a lie, because the

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:33.080
truth is you almost always get caught.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.880
Goldman's culture was like intense, and

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.040
I wasn't there for very long, but you

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.000
knew where you stood, and you had a

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:39.840
choice. Own it and course correct or

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.880
become a victim and start trying again.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.840
Literally every person who I've watched

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.360
choose to lie instead of course correct,

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.320
well, they never came back from it. The

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.360
ones who owned their failures, some of

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.160
them went on to do huge things, because

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.520
people remember they were honest under

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:58.440
pressure. So, as my dad says, tell the

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.440
truth even when it's going to cost you.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.240
Number four is what people think about

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.919
you is actually your asset. You should

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.840
treat it like one. "I don't care what

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.880
people think" is one of the most

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:12.480
expensive lies you can tell yourself.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.000
And I actually remember Goldman learning

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.760
that the hard way during the the

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.480
financial crisis. They weren't the

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.160
primary villain of the collapse,

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.000
actually. It was like Countrywide and

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.320
predatory mortgage originators. They

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.560
were writing these terrible loans at

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.400
scale, not Goldman. So, why did Goldman

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.360
get absolutely destroyed in the court of

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.480
public opinion? Because they were really

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.640
bad at one thing. They let other people

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.720
write their narrative, and they let it

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.480
be wrong. And it cost them billions in

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.440
reputations, and relationships, and

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.520
deals that probably never happened, and

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:42.240
talent, you know, that went elsewhere.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.040
Your version, yeah, maybe it's not

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.760
billions at this point, but it's just as

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.800
real. If people carry a story about you,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:51.160
deserved or not, that you're unreliable,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.560
difficult, someone who cuts corners, the

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.040
introductions that could have changed

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.240
your life, well, they don't even get

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.640
made because nobody's going to announce

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.880
that they're not going to do something

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.680
big for you. So, the opportunity just

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.480
goes to somebody else. Your reputation

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.800
is an asset, and like any asset, it

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.120
either compounds or it's going to decay

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.480
depending on how much attention you pay

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.080
to it. Think hard about how you show up,

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:15.000
what you say publicly, what story people

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.680
tell you the moment you leave the room.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.960
What do people say about you? Because

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.480
the story is being written whether

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.440
you're involved or not. The only

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:24.680
question is whether you actually wrote

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.680
the thing or not.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.360
Lesson number five is stop trying to get

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.400
into easy doors. I went through

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.760
somewhere between seven and 12

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.880
interviews to get into this place,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:38.840
Goldman. They make it brutally hard on

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.640
purpose, but once you understand why, it

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.680
changes how you go after any other

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.040
opportunity in life. Like, you had to

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:47.000
fly to headquarters in person, prepare a

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.520
full presentation and deliver it live,

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:50.080
then sit through the role plays where

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.000
partners challenge your assumptions in

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.080
real time. Like, you said this was going

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.760
to happen. I don't believe you. Back it

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.640
up. And you either have an answer or you

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.880
didn't. And, you know, back in the day,

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.480
I beat out hundreds of candidates for

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.200
that seat, and I was just a little

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.840
starting role analyst. But Goldman on

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.840
your resume is kind of like a skeleton

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:08.160
key. It can get you in just about

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.160
anywhere later in life. People even

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.840
bring that up about me now despite all

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.600
my other accomplishments. Like, the

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.400
harder it is to get into something, the

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.400
more valuable it is to be inside, and

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.760
that's not an accident. So, I see too

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.640
many people today trying to get into

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:23.280
easy doors because they get you easy

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.600
outcomes. If somebody right now is

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.400
trying to apply for a job, if you're

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.800
trying to apply for a job, the rooms

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.640
that are actually hard to access are

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:32.840
where your trajectory changes. So, go.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.560
Do like 15 interviews for a place. Do a

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:36.240
project. Do hard work. Don't listen to

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.680
the people on the internet that tell you

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.360
not to do that. Most people do not try

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.480
hard enough to get where they are. They

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.560
send a bunch of applications, do one

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.880
interview, call it that an attempt, and

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.120
that is absolute lunacy. You have to

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.320
outwork hundreds of people for a single

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:54.000
seat, and I would do it again tomorrow.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.480
I did it back in the day because the

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.560
more exclusive that door is, the more

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:00.680
worth it to throw yourself at it

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.480
repeatedly without embarrassment until

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:06.880
you get it cuz once you open

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:06.880
they can never take that back.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:12.080
>> [music]

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.720
>> Number six, duration of work beats

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.040
output of work, especially when you're

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:18.000
young and building. You know, because

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.960
you're behind. When you walk into

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.960
Goldman Sachs as a public school kid,

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.080
like, you know, me, I realized pretty

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.240
quickly I was in a room full of people

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.280
who've been preparing for this their

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.440
whole lives, and so like, you know, they

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.680
knew the vocabulary. They'd summered in

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.360
places, and I didn't even know what

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:36.080
summering, you know, was as a verb. They

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.880
use shorthand like, um, lezzing, which

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.599
means leverage finance. I didn't even

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.440
know what leverage finance was. So,

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:42.960
like, rich people build a language that

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.880
keeps other people out, whether by

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.839
design or not. And you have to learn to

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.600
just play. And in order to play, you got

00:08:47.839 --> 00:08:51.920
to get fluent if you actually want to

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:54.560
win. I couldn't out-credential or

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:56.240
out-network them. Duration was the only

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.640
lever I had, another fancy word for

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.800
saying like, can I do this for a really

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:02.400
long time? And so, you know, when I was

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.839
starting at Goldman, I was the first one

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:06.200
in and the last one out every day for

00:09:04.839 --> 00:09:07.839
most of my first year, at least in our

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.480
small little group. And I remember there

00:09:07.839 --> 00:09:11.200
was a managing director in our group,

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:13.120
his name was Jim. He was a former

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.560
military, super hardcore, and barely

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.080
talked to any of us. He was in at 6:30

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.560
in the morning. So, I made sure I was

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:20.200
there before him, and I was there when

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.480
he left. And eventually he noticed and

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.760
started giving me like shot after shot

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.920
that nobody else got. And so, that meant

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.280
I got more exposure and more

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:31.000
opportunities because he saw I was

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:33.120
willing to work. I was hungry. So, I

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.800
watched this play out the same way at my

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.120
own company. Now, you know, we just

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.400
promoted someone who is not the most

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.040
credentialed person in the building, but

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:40.840
he's always in earlier and out later

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.400
than everyone else and building cool,

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:44.480
weird side projects. It's not like he's

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.680
just wasting his time. And he makes more

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.280
money than people who've been here

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.680
longer. Because when you're young and

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.360
behind, duration is probably your only

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.960
lever. Like, you're not smarter than

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.720
other people, perhaps. You're not more

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:57.640
experienced, but you can work harder.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:00.400
And so, the question is, that door,

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:02.000
maybe it's not open right now, but it

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:06.840
will be when you do the things you have

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.840
to to get noticed. That's what I found.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.560
This last meeting really reminds me of

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:16.680
number seven. Do not brown nose mediocre

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.760
people. It just makes you mediocre. I

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.800
mean, you want to pick the right person

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.320
to impress. And that is a skill that can

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.920
change your life. Yet, most people are

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.480
terrible at it. Like, when I was at

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:27.240
Goldman, we had multiple managers and

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:28.960
the contrast between two of them made

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.800
this lesson like impossible for me to

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.920
miss. So, Jim, who I mentioned earlier,

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.080
was serious, respected. And then there

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:37.040
was, you know, this other guy. I'll be

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.920
kind. And I won't say his name. But I

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.839
remember, you can just picture this. He

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:43.000
once opened a team meeting by showing us

00:10:40.839 --> 00:10:45.120
a video of himself as a kid kickboxing

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.480
in a ring. Like, he had Rocky music

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.360
playing and I think he was trying to

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.360
inspire us like during the financial

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:51.520
crisis. I'm not really sure. I mean, I'm

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.600
sitting there thinking, what is

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.520
happening right now? And the way he

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:57.080
walked was like Weekend at Bernie's

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.480
every other day, but now I'm being mean.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:01.200
But like, he is completely unserious in

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:03.720
a place that rewards nothing but

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.400
seriousness. Now, one woman on our team

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.120
figured out he was easy to work. Like,

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:10.080
she brought him a birthday cake on his

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.080
birthday and the two of them weirdly ate

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.320
it alone in his office. Like, super

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:16.480
weird energy. However, like, climbed up

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:18.440
within our relative group pretty quickly

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:21.120
under him. But none of the real leaders

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:23.040
at Goldman took him or her seriously and

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.440
her path went nowhere because of that.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.360
So, like, the real players there had

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.280
zero interest in being flattered. And

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:31.680
that is true everywhere. You cannot

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:33.560
charm your way into their sphere. And

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.160
the tell is really this. If you can BS

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:37.160
your way into somebody's good graces,

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.280
they're not worth being your champion.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:41.440
What it takes to get into the winner's

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.120
sphere is exactly what makes them worth

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.400
being in. Like, if you're awesome and

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.960
exceptional, the real players, they come

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.720
to you. And that's the whole point of

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.640
the game. So, don't feel like you have

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.080
to brown-nose people that you don't even

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.520
want to shake hands with. You know what

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.520
I'm saying?

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:00.200
You know,

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:02.720
I think you got to make yourself so

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:04.960
valuable that you write your own terms.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:06.840
So, that's number eight. A colleague at

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.680
Goldman actually taught me so much about

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.080
how to create freedom for yourself. Her

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.320
name was Gailey. She's a badass. She's

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.240
actually on the internet, but she's more

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:15.560
senior than I was. She was running an

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.880
entire interior design business on the

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.920
side with an HGTV show and a massive

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:21.480
Instagram following, all while talking

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.480
publicly about Goldman.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.800
And Goldman let her do all of it because

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.840
she was really good at her job. You

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.960
know, compare that to someone who you

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:32.080
know feels stifled, constrained, like

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:34.200
the company has a stranglehold on what

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.960
they're allowed to do. If you feel that

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.880
constricted, you're probably not

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:40.440
valuable enough yet. And that's like the

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:42.960
honest, tough read. You know, Goldman

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:44.240
does the anti-sell in recruiting. Like,

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.520
they're like, "If you're not willing to

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.480
work harder than you've ever worked in

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:49.600
your life, don't come here." And I

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:51.600
respect that clarity. This is a little

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:54.080
dramatic, but kind of true. I didn't see

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.520
the sun for, I think, my entire first

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.360
winter there. I was actually in Chicago.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:00.280
And that's just what it cost. But, if

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.880
you're crushing it, nobody cares when

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:03.240
you come or go. You do what you want.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:05.080
You come in when you want. You leave

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:07.560
when you want because output when you're

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:10.600
a winner is the only metric. So, get

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:14.040
that right. Freedom comes after it. I've

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:14.040
seen that time and again.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:20.800
Number nine, actually, the partnership

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.560
model is the real wealth vehicle. And

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.839
our

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.760
entire generation is asleep on it. So,

00:13:23.839 --> 00:13:28.080
like, this company, Goldman, has been

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.400
making its partners extraordinarily rich

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:33.160
for 150 years. And almost none of it

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:35.120
came from an IPO or one huge exit. The

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:36.720
most profitable law firms in the world

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:38.480
run the same way. So does like private

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:40.000
equity, top insurance companies,

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:42.000
investment managers, Wall Street

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.600
broadly. They're all partnerships. And

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.120
like stick with me here for a while.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:48.400
Like startup culture sold our entire

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:50.440
generation on equity. Get in early, you

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:52.160
know, get the equity, pray for the exit,

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:53.920
and maybe you'll get rich. But most

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:55.920
people actually don't. So the math on

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:57.920
startup equity is bad for most people

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:59.840
who play it. And I've done the opposite

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.960
my whole career. Like every major

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:03.560
business I've built has been a

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:05.200
partnership. And you can make

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:07.400
extraordinary money in them even as

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:09.080
employees. The Goldman partners I

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.200
watched, they didn't get rich from

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:12.960
salary alone. They had bonuses and

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:15.080
decades of personal investments all

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:18.240
compounding at once. I know a guy at

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:20.080
BlackRock who makes $500 million in a

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:22.080
single year. And that comes from

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:24.480
compounding partnership economics over

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:27.080
time and doing deals. So when you

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.280
optimize for upside tied to performance,

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:31.240
not equity in somebody else's company,

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.640
you can make a lot more. So I like to

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:35.000
ask myself this question. You can steal

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:37.040
it. If I do X, I make more, and that's

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:39.120
the structure you want. And where the

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:42.200
actual upside comes from when you're

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:44.000
starting out is risking a little bit and

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.570
betting on yourself. At least that's

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.590
what I think.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.590
>> [music]

00:14:47.839 --> 00:14:53.000
>> Number 10. Start investing in off-market

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:54.800
deals right now with whatever you have.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:56.080
Like Goldman taught me that the real

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.520
money to be made was never in the

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:59.600
salary. It was always in the deals on

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.839
the side. So Goldman, everybody always

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:02.480
talked about where they were putting

00:15:00.839 --> 00:15:04.640
their own money, not inside of their

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.880
job, but outside of it. So, you know, a

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.880
colleague might be like buying a duplex.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.160
A company might be raising a small round

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:13.560
from people in the company. And, you

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:15.440
know, you pool whatever you have, and

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:17.040
early on that might not be much, but it

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:18.240
was something. So my first couple of

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.800
deals might have been a couple of

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:24.160
thousand bucks. But they were small and

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:25.520
those compounded into big. I mean, some

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:27.240
of my first outside investments I think

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:29.280
were duplexes, real estate deals with

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:30.920
people I actually knew, then an outdoor

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.400
advertising startup that was a couple

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:34.840
hundred K that made me that was a lot

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.320
back then, then a fortune cookie

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:38.400
advertising company of all things that

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:40.560
also made us a couple hundred K. And I

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:43.000
put real money into like this random

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:45.000
fortune cookie company, but I put it to

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:46.320
people in my network that I trusted. And

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.720
every time I did one of those deals, I

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:50.600
was like building that muscle, you know?

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:52.520
Like Goldman partners at the top, they

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:55.600
get rich by investing in deal after deal

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:57.320
alongside their salary for decades.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.840
Nobody in our generation is investing

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:02.320
aggressively enough. Or they're like

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:03.520
Robinhood YOLO-ing it into crypto. And I

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:05.640
think that's probably because the story

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.400
most of us tell ourselves is that you

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.680
need real money to start. You only need

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:11.720
a thousand, five thousand, 10K, whatever

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:14.840
you can set aside. And you just start

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:16.600
putting it into the people around you.

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:18.920
Most people work only talk about work,

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:20.760
not about your outside investments. And

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:22.840
I think that's the problem today. The

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:25.200
deals you're walking past every day will

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:26.600
shock you, which is exactly why I built

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:28.720
Main Street Millionaire LIVE. It's

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:30.480
basically a three-day event in June

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:33.360
where we show you how to actually act on

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:35.320
that idea. Totally virtual, but you find

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.920
off-market businesses, you know, how to

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:39.040
fund a deal without draining all your

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:41.080
savings, how to structure it so a seller

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.920
picks you over private equity who maybe

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:44.720
is writing a bigger check. And these are

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.520
real deals, real numbers. And tickets

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:48.040
are less than a couple cups of coffee.

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.720
You can join from anywhere in the world

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:51.640
virtually. The people from last year's

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.800
event like they aren't just talking

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:54.760
about investing anymore, they're

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.160
actually closing. So actually, I'm going

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:58.560
to throw a link in the description for

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:00.520
this for you guys because working inside

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:02.520
that building at Goldman was one of the

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:04.760
most intense crash courses in wealth

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:06.839
creation that existed for me. Most

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:09.280
people never get access. Part of my goal

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:10.600
is to give that all to you guys. Now I'm

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:12.680
off to dinner to hopefully do another

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:14.199
deal, but I hope you've stolen the

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.079
lessons today. Tell me which one your

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:18.120
favorite one was. Maybe we'll do another

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:20.640
video on it. You found the new in your

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:20.640
hands.

